1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,444
Eight gigabytes of RAM.

2
00:00:01,444 --> 00:00:02,700
Integrated graphics.

3
00:00:02,700 --> 00:00:04,646
No network, the ping proves it.

4
00:00:04,646 --> 00:00:07,157
Everything you see runs on this machine.

5
00:00:08,400 --> 00:00:12,148
Eighty percent of Ivorian firms report power cuts.

6
00:00:12,148 --> 00:00:13,872
A laptop has a battery;

7
00:00:13,872 --> 00:00:15,446
a fibre box does not.

8
00:00:15,446 --> 00:00:22,193
So the assistant answers here, one of our two declared prompts, generated now, on the CPU.

9
00:00:22,700 --> 00:00:26,107
The same model, reachable by USSD from any phone.

10
00:00:26,107 --> 00:00:31,045
No data plan, no app, one hundred and eighty-two characters per screen.

11
00:00:31,045 --> 00:00:34,174
The order file it reads is written in French;

12
00:00:34,174 --> 00:00:37,929
the answer comes back in English, on the same machine.

13
00:00:38,400 --> 00:00:40,271
The documents stay on the disk.

14
00:00:40,271 --> 00:00:46,308
On screen you just saw about twenty-seven tokens per second, because recording the screen costs CPU.

15
00:00:46,308 --> 00:00:50,172
Measured clean with the official profiler, median of three runs:

16
00:00:50,172 --> 00:00:56,451
thirty-one point two tokens per second, one point five gigabytes peak, against a seven gigabyte ceiling.

17
00:00:57,000 --> 00:00:59,797
Getting here was measurement, not intuition.

18
00:00:59,797 --> 00:01:04,757
We profiled five open base models, then all seven quantisations of the winner.

19
00:01:04,757 --> 00:01:08,381
One decision we had already published, we later reversed:

20
00:01:08,381 --> 00:01:12,386
a second control showed the first had measured the wrong thing.

21
00:01:12,900 --> 00:01:17,670
Every number in our report is measured, including the ones that do not flatter us.

22
00:01:17,670 --> 00:01:21,102
The repository names the variant that beats us on accuracy.

